      en: "Basra, the strategically vital port city in southeastern Iraq, is located on the Shatt al-Arab waterway at the confluence of the Euphrates and Tigris rivers near the Persian Gulf. Founded in 636 AD, the city played a pivotal role in early Islamic history as a major commercial and theological center. Historically, Basra was famous for its extensive network of canals, earning it the nickname \"Venice of the East.\" Today, the city serves as the logistics hub for Iraq's petroleum industry, with the nation's largest oil fields located in its vicinity and nearly all exports flowing through its maritime terminals. The climate is characterized by extreme heat and humidity, particularly during the summer months. Despite suffering significant damage during the Iran-Iraq War and subsequent conflicts, Basra has preserved elements of its cultural heritage, such as the traditional \"Shanasheel\" houses featuring ornate wooden balconies. The city remains globally renowned for its high-quality date exports.",
